---
title: ':bread: Olive and Onion Fougasse'
source: https://www.kingarthurbaking.com/recipes/olive-and-onion-fougasse-recipe
prep time: 22 minutes
cook time: 20 minutes
total time: 11 hours 12 minutes
tags:
  - baking
  - bread
  - french
  - king-arthur-baking
  - savory
servings: 1
---

To make the poolish, combine @bread flour{1%cup}, @water{1/2%cup}, and @instant yeast{1/8%tsp} in a #bowl{}. Cover and set aside to rest overnight.

To prepare the filling, combine @butter{1%Tbsp} and @diced onion{1%cup} in a #pan{} set over medium-low heat. Cook for about ~{20%minutes}, stirring every 5 minutes, until nicely caramelized. Transfer the onions to a bowl and cool to room temperature before using.

To make the dough, add @water{1/2%cup}, @bread flour{1%cup}, and @whole wheat flour{1%cup} to the poolish and mix until shaggy. Cover the bowl and allow the mixture to rest for ~{20%minutes}.

Add @instant yeast{2%tsp}, @table salt{1%tsp}, and @olive oil{2%Tbsp}, and knead the dough briefly until it forms a shaggy mass. Mix in the caramelized onions and roughly chopped pitted @Kalamata olives{1/2%cup}, and knead until fairly supple but not necessarily elastic, about ~{3%minutes} with a #stand mixer{} or 5 minutes by hand.

Place the dough in a lightly greased bowl, cover, and allow it to rise at room temperature for ~{1.5%hours}, gently deflating it and turning it over after 45 minutes.

Shape the dough into a 12-inch by 6-inch oval, place it on a lightly greased #baking sheet{}, and allow it to rise for about ~{30%minutes}.

Using a sharp #knife{} or pair of scissors, cut a single slit all the way through the dough lengthwise down the center, stopping about 1 inch from each end. Follow up with three or four diagonal slits on each side of the center slit.

To create a leaf-like appearance, open up each slit by gently stretching the dough with your fingers, leaving behind holes that are at least 2 inches wide so the leaf design remains visible after rising.

Allow the fougasse to rise until noticeably puffy, 30 to ~{45%minutes}.

Toward the end of the rising time, preheat the #oven{} to 400°F. For an extra-crispy crust, place a baking stone on the center rack.

Bake the fougasse for ~{20%minutes} or until it turns golden brown. Remove from the oven and transfer to a #cooling rack{} to cool.

Store well-wrapped at room temperature for up to 3 days, or freeze for up to 1 month.
